-- Q1: hard / L4 — Evaluate self-rental recharacterization impact on tax planning
(6, 'Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules', 'Crawford, a sole proprietor, operates a consulting firm from a building she owns personally. The building generates $48,000 of rental income, and Crawford has $62,000 of suspended passive losses from an unrelated limited partnership. Her CPA suggests increasing the rent to generate more passive income that would absorb the suspended losses. Evaluate the effectiveness of this strategy under the self-rental recharacterization rules.', '["The strategy is ineffective because rental income from property leased to a taxpayer''s own business is recharacterized as nonpassive under Treas. Reg. 1.469-2(f)(6), so it cannot absorb suspended passive losses", "The strategy is effective because rental income is always classified as passive regardless of the identity of the tenant", "The strategy is partially effective because only rent exceeding fair market value is recharacterized as nonpassive", "The strategy is ineffective because self-rental income is treated as portfolio income excluded from passive activity calculations"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under IRC Section 469 and Treas. Reg. 1.469-2(f)(6), rental income from property leased to a trade or business in which the taxpayer materially participates is recharacterized as nonpassive income. This prevents taxpayers from generating artificial passive income to offset suspended passive losses. Choice B is incorrect because the self-rental rule is a specific exception to the general classification of rental income as passive.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q2: hard / L3 — Analyze related-party sale and suspended passive loss release
(6, 'Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules', 'Rivera has $85,000 in suspended passive losses from a rental property with a basis of $150,000 and FMV of $220,000. Rivera sells the property to his brother, Delgado, for $220,000 in an arm''s-length transaction. Analyze the impact of this disposition on Rivera''s suspended passive losses.', '["All $85,000 of suspended losses are released because the transaction was at fair market value", "The $85,000 of suspended losses remain suspended until Delgado disposes of the property to an unrelated party", "The suspended losses are permanently disallowed due to the related-party transaction", "Only $70,000 of suspended losses equal to the gain on sale are released, with $15,000 remaining suspended"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under IRC Section 469(g)(1)(B), a disposition to a related party as defined under Section 267(b) does not trigger the release of suspended passive losses. Brothers are related parties under Section 267(b)(1). The losses remain suspended until the related buyer disposes of the property to an unrelated party. Choice A is incorrect because the arm''s-length price is irrelevant — the controlling factor is the related-party relationship, not the sale price.', 'hard', 3),

-- Q3: medium / L3 — Analyze publicly traded partnership passive loss quarantine
(6, 'Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules', 'Nakamura owns interests in two passive activities during 2026. Activity X is a publicly traded partnership that generates a $28,000 loss. Activity Y is a private limited partnership that generates $35,000 of passive income. Nakamura has no other passive activities. Analyze how the PTP loss interacts with the private partnership income.', '["The $28,000 PTP loss offsets the $35,000 passive income from Activity Y, leaving $7,000 of net passive income", "The $28,000 PTP loss is deductible against Nakamura''s ordinary income because PTP losses are exempt from passive limitations", "The $28,000 PTP loss is suspended and can only be offset against future income from the same PTP, leaving the $35,000 of passive income fully taxable", "The PTP loss and private partnership income are netted, but only 50% of the PTP loss is allowable against non-PTP passive income"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under IRC Section 469(k), losses from a publicly traded partnership may only be offset against income from the same PTP. PTP losses cannot be netted against passive income from other sources. The $28,000 loss is suspended and carries forward until Nakamura has income from that same PTP or disposes of the interest. Choice A is incorrect because the netting rules for non-PTP passive activities do not extend to PTP losses, which are quarantined by activity.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q4: hard / L4 — Evaluate electing out of installment method for passive activity sale
(6, 'Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules', 'Gallagher plans to sell her entire interest in a passive activity for $400,000, generating a $160,000 gain. She has $145,000 in suspended passive losses from this activity. The buyer proposes a 4-year installment arrangement with equal annual payments. Gallagher''s CPA notes that electing out of the installment method under Section 453(d) would require recognizing the full $160,000 gain in the year of sale. Evaluate whether Gallagher should elect out of the installment method.', '["She should not elect out because the installment method spreads the gain over four years, reducing the peak marginal rate", "She should not elect out because suspended passive losses are released proportionally under either method with no timing advantage", "She should elect out because recognizing the full gain converts the suspended losses from passive to portfolio deductions", "She should elect out because full gain recognition triggers the complete release of all $145,000 in suspended losses under Section 469(g)(1), producing a net taxable gain of only $15,000 in the current year"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under IRC Section 469(g)(1), a fully taxable disposition of an entire passive activity interest to an unrelated party releases all suspended passive losses. By electing out of the installment method under Section 453(d), Gallagher recognizes the full $160,000 gain and triggers immediate release of all $145,000 in suspended losses, netting only $15,000 of taxable gain. Choice A is incorrect because while installment reporting spreads the gain, it also proportionally defers the loss release, eliminating the immediate offset benefit.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q5: easy / L1 — Definition of rental activity under Section 469
(6, 'Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules', 'Under IRC Section 469(j)(8), which characteristic defines an activity as a rental activity?', '["The activity involves providing services to customers for a fee", "The activity involves paying tangible property costs exceeding $50,000 annually", "The activity involves providing tangible property for customer use where income is principally from payments for such use", "The activity generates primarily portfolio income from real property investments"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under IRC Section 469(j)(8), a rental activity is one where payments are principally for the use of tangible property. Rental activities are generally treated as per se passive regardless of the taxpayer''s level of participation, subject to specific exceptions. Choice A is incorrect because providing services for a fee describes a trade or business activity, not a rental activity, though certain rental exceptions apply when extraordinary personal services are provided.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q6: medium / L2 — Apply former passive activity loss rules
(6, 'Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules', 'Thornton invested in a catering business as a passive investor from 2022 through 2025, accumulating $42,000 in suspended passive losses. In 2026, Thornton begins working full-time in the business, logging 1,400 hours of participation. The business generates $18,000 of income in 2026. Under the former passive activity rules, how are Thornton''s suspended losses treated?', '["The $42,000 of suspended losses remain passive and can only offset passive income from other activities", "The suspended losses of $18,000 are allowed against the 2026 business income, with $24,000 remaining as a carryforward deductible only against future income from this activity", "All $42,000 of suspended losses are immediately deductible against Thornton''s nonpassive income in 2026", "The $42,000 of suspended losses are permanently disallowed because the activity changed classification"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under IRC Section 469(f)(1), when a former passive activity becomes nonpassive through material participation, suspended losses are allowed against income from that activity. In 2026, $18,000 of the suspended losses offsets the $18,000 of activity income. The remaining $24,000 carries forward and is deductible only against future income from this same former passive activity. Choice C is incorrect because former passive losses are not fully released against all nonpassive income — they are limited to income from the specific former passive activity.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q7: medium / L3 — Analyze three-tier loss limitation ordering (basis, at-risk, passive)
(6, 'Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules', 'Patel, a 30% member of Silverline Ventures LLC, has a tax basis of $55,000 and an at-risk amount of $40,000. Patel does not materially participate. In 2026, Silverline allocates $60,000 of ordinary losses to Patel. Analyze how the three-tier loss limitation ordering affects Patel''s deductible loss.', '["Patel deducts $40,000 because the at-risk amount is the most restrictive limitation", "Patel deducts $55,000 because LLC members are exempt from at-risk limitations under Section 465", "Patel deducts nothing because the passive activity rules apply first and block the entire loss before other rules are considered", "Patel deducts nothing currently: the $60,000 loss is reduced to $55,000 by the basis limitation, then to $40,000 by at-risk rules, and the $40,000 is fully suspended by passive activity rules"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Loss deductions follow a strict ordering under the IRC: basis limitation (Section 704(d) for partnerships), at-risk rules (Section 465), then passive activity rules (Section 469). Patel''s $60,000 loss is reduced to $55,000 by basis, then to $40,000 by at-risk rules, and the $40,000 is suspended as a passive loss because Patel does not materially participate. Choice C is incorrect because passive rules are the last tier applied, not the first — the ordering is basis, at-risk, then passive.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q8: medium / L2 — Calculate at-risk amount with personal guarantee of recourse debt
(6, 'Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules', 'Okoye invests $75,000 cash in Clearwater Enterprises LLC and personally guarantees a $120,000 bank loan to the LLC. No other member guarantees the loan. Under the at-risk rules of Section 465, what is Okoye''s at-risk amount?', '["$195,000, calculated as $75,000 cash plus the full $120,000 loan for which Okoye has personal liability", "$123,000, calculated as $75,000 cash plus 40% of the $120,000 loan based on ownership share", "$75,000, because debt for which another party could also be liable is excluded from the at-risk computation", "$120,000, because only the personally liable debt amount determines the at-risk amount"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under IRC Section 465(b)(1)-(2), a taxpayer''s at-risk amount includes cash contributions plus amounts borrowed for which the taxpayer has personal liability. Because Okoye personally guarantees the full $120,000 loan, the entire amount for which Okoye has personal liability is included regardless of ownership percentage. At-risk = $75,000 + $120,000 = $195,000. Choice B is incorrect because the at-risk rules measure personal liability, not proportionate ownership share — the full liability amount creates personal exposure.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q9: medium / L2 — Calculate suspended passive loss carryforward after partial netting
(6, 'Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules', 'Foster has $95,000 in accumulated suspended passive losses from a limited partnership. During 2026, Foster receives $38,000 of passive income from a rental property and $12,000 of passive income from another investment. Foster has no other passive income or losses in 2026. What is Foster''s suspended passive loss carryforward at the end of 2026?', '["$95,000, because suspended losses from one activity cannot offset income from a different passive activity", "$57,000, because $38,000 of passive income is applied but the $12,000 is classified as portfolio income", "$45,000, calculated as $95,000 minus the combined $50,000 of passive income", "$83,000, because only the rental income can absorb limited partnership losses"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under IRC Section 469(d), passive losses from all activities are netted against passive income from all activities in aggregate. Foster''s $95,000 of suspended passive losses absorbs the combined $50,000 of passive income ($38,000 + $12,000), leaving $45,000 in suspended passive losses carried forward to 2027. Choice A is incorrect because passive activity netting is performed in aggregate across all passive activities, not on a per-activity basis.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q10: easy / L1 — Working interest exception for oil and gas
(6, 'Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules', 'Under IRC Section 469(c)(3), which type of interest in an oil and gas activity is exempt from the passive activity loss rules?', '["A royalty interest held by a nonoperating investor", "A working interest held through an entity that does not limit the taxpayer''s personal liability", "Any interest in an oil and gas partnership regardless of liability structure", "A carried interest in an oil and gas venture funded entirely by other partners"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under IRC Section 469(c)(3), a working interest in an oil and gas property is exempt from passive activity limitations provided the taxpayer holds the interest through a form that does not limit personal liability (e.g., as a general partner or sole proprietor). This exception applies regardless of participation level. Choice A is incorrect because a royalty interest is not a working interest — royalty holders do not bear operational costs and remain subject to standard passive activity rules.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q11: medium / L2 — Apply 10% ownership requirement for active participation
(6, 'Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules', 'Brennan owns a 7% interest in a rental apartment complex through a general partnership. Brennan approves tenants, sets rental terms, and authorizes capital expenditures. Brennan''s AGI is $85,000 and the rental generates a $30,000 loss, of which Brennan''s share is $2,100. How much of Brennan''s rental loss qualifies for the $25,000 active participation allowance?', '["$2,100, because Brennan actively participates by making management decisions", "$2,100, but only because Brennan''s AGI is below $100,000", "$0, because the total rental loss exceeds the $25,000 allowance", "$0, because active participation requires at least a 10% ownership interest in the activity"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under IRC Section 469(i)(6)(A), the $25,000 active participation rental loss allowance requires the taxpayer to own at least 10% of the activity by value. Despite Brennan''s management involvement, the 7% ownership interest disqualifies Brennan from the active participation exception. Choice A is incorrect because making management decisions alone is insufficient — the 10% ownership threshold is a separate, independent requirement that must also be met.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q12: medium / L3 — Analyze 7-day average rental period exception
(6, 'Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules', 'Yamamoto owns a beachfront vacation property rented through an online platform. In 2026, the property is rented for 140 total days across 28 separate bookings, averaging 5 days per guest. Yamamoto spends 820 hours managing the property, handling guest communications, and performing maintenance. Analyze how the average rental period affects the passive activity classification.', '["The property is a rental activity because total rental days exceed 100, making it per se passive regardless of hours", "The property is a rental activity because Yamamoto does not qualify as a real estate professional", "The property is not a rental activity under the 7-day exception, and because Yamamoto spends 820 hours managing it, the income and losses are nonpassive", "The property is classified as a rental activity, but the 820 hours automatically qualify Yamamoto for the $25,000 active participation allowance"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under IRC Section 469 and Treas. Reg. 1.469-1T(e)(3)(ii)(A), an activity is not treated as a rental activity if the average period of customer use is 7 days or less. With a 5-day average, Yamamoto''s property falls under this exception and is treated as a trade or business. Because Yamamoto spends 820 hours, exceeding the 500-hour material participation test, the activity is nonpassive. Choice A is incorrect because total rental days are irrelevant; the 7-day exception turns on average customer use period, not aggregate duration.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q13: easy / L1 — Scope of at-risk rules under Section 465
(6, 'Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules', 'Under IRC Section 465, which taxpayers are generally subject to the at-risk rules?', '["Individuals and closely held C corporations engaged in any activity", "Only taxpayers engaged in farming and oil and gas exploration", "Only S corporation shareholders with nonrecourse debt", "Only limited partners in real estate partnerships"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under IRC Section 465(a), the at-risk rules apply to individuals and closely held C corporations engaged in any trade or business or income-producing activity. The rules were originally limited to specific activities (farming, oil and gas, movies, leasing) but were expanded in 1978 to cover virtually all activities. Choice B is incorrect because while those were among the original targeted activities, the at-risk rules now apply broadly to all activities.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q14: easy / L1 — Personal service corporation passive loss treatment
(6, 'Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules', 'Under IRC Section 469(a)(2), how are passive activity losses treated for a personal service corporation?', '["Passive losses may offset the corporation''s active business income without limitation", "Passive losses are permanently disallowed in the year incurred", "Passive losses may only offset the corporation''s portfolio income", "Passive losses may only offset the corporation''s passive income"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under IRC Section 469(a)(2)(B), a personal service corporation may deduct passive losses only against passive income. Unlike closely held C corporations, personal service corporations cannot offset passive losses against active business income. This stricter rule prevents high-earning professionals from sheltering service income with passive deductions. Choice A is incorrect because the active income offset is available only to closely held C corporations that are not personal service corporations, under Section 469(e)(2).', 'easy', 1),

-- Q15: easy / L2 — Apply 500-hour material participation test
(6, 'Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules', 'Kessler owns a 50% interest in a landscape design business. During 2026, Kessler logs 520 hours working on design consultations, client meetings, and project oversight. Kessler''s partner logs 1,200 hours. Under the Section 469 regulations, does Kessler materially participate?', '["No, because Kessler''s 520 hours are less than half of the partner''s 1,200 hours", "Yes, because Kessler participated for more than 500 hours during the tax year", "No, because material participation requires the taxpayer to have the most hours of any participant", "Yes, but only if Kessler also participates on a regular, continuous, and substantial basis"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under IRC Section 469 and Treas. Reg. 1.469-5T(a)(1), a taxpayer materially participates if the individual participates for more than 500 hours during the tax year. This is a standalone test — meeting 500 hours is sufficient regardless of how many hours others participate. Choice A is incorrect because the 500-hour test does not compare the taxpayer''s hours to other participants; it is an absolute threshold that Kessler exceeds.', 'easy', 2),

-- Q16: medium / L2 — Calculate passive credit limitation against passive tax
(6, 'Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules', 'Callahan receives $14,000 of tax credits from a passive trade or business activity in 2026. Callahan has $8,000 of tax attributable to net passive income from other activities and no active participation in any rental. Determine the treatment of the $14,000 in passive credits.', '["The full $14,000 credit is applied against Callahan''s total tax liability regardless of passive income", "The $14,000 credit is permanently lost because it exceeds the tax on passive income", "Only $8,000 of credits offsets the tax on passive income, and the remaining $6,000 is carried forward to future years", "The $14,000 credit is converted to a $14,000 passive loss deduction"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under IRC Section 469(d)(2), passive activity credits may only offset tax attributable to passive income. Credits exceeding this amount are not lost but are suspended and carried forward to future years when the taxpayer has sufficient passive income tax liability. Choice B is incorrect because suspended passive credits are not permanently disallowed; like passive losses, they carry forward indefinitely until utilized or the activity is disposed of.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q17: hard / L4 — Evaluate impact of reclassifying passive income source as nonpassive
(6, 'Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules', 'Reeves, a tax consultant, has three investments: a bakery limited partnership generating $35,000 annual losses with $90,000 suspended losses, a car wash in which Reeves participates 450 hours generating $20,000 income, and a bookstore generating $15,000 losses with 100 hours of participation. Reeves considers increasing car wash participation to 550 hours to achieve material participation. Evaluate the tax consequence of this change.', '["Reclassifying the car wash has no effect because net passive losses remain the same regardless of classification", "Reclassifying the car wash as nonpassive is harmful because the $20,000 of passive income currently absorbing passive losses would become nonpassive income no longer available for passive netting", "Reclassifying the car wash is beneficial because the income would then be exempt from self-employment tax", "Reclassifying the car wash is beneficial because remaining passive losses would offset the bookstore losses more efficiently"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under IRC Section 469(d)(1), passive losses may only be deducted against passive income. Currently, the car wash''s $20,000 passive income partially offsets the combined $50,000 of passive losses ($35,000 bakery + $15,000 bookstore), suspending only $30,000. If Reeves achieves material participation, the car wash becomes nonpassive, removing $20,000 from the passive pool and increasing suspended losses to $50,000. Choice D is incorrect because removing passive income from the netting pool reduces the ability to utilize passive losses.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q18: easy / L1 — Passive loss treatment for personal service corporations vs. closely held C corps
(6, 'Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules', 'Under IRC Section 469(e)(2), how may a closely held C corporation that is not a personal service corporation use its passive activity losses?', '["Passive losses may offset active business income but not portfolio income", "Passive losses may offset only passive income, identical to personal service corporation rules", "Passive losses may offset both active business income and portfolio income without restriction", "Passive losses are permanently disallowed for all C corporations"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under IRC Section 469(e)(2), a closely held C corporation that is not a personal service corporation may offset passive losses against net active income from trade or business activities. However, passive losses still cannot offset portfolio income (interest, dividends, capital gains). Choice B is incorrect because the closely held C corporation rule is less restrictive than the personal service corporation rule — PSCs may only offset passive income, while non-PSC closely held corporations may also offset active business income.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q19: medium / L3 — Analyze net investment income tax interaction with passive income
(6, 'Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules', 'Hoffman has $145,000 in W-2 wages, $60,000 of passive rental income, and $25,000 of passive losses from a limited partnership. Hoffman''s MAGI is $280,000 and filing status is single with a $200,000 threshold. Analyze how the passive income affects Hoffman''s net investment income tax liability under Section 1411.', '["The $35,000 net passive income is included in NII, and the 3.8% NIIT applies to $35,000 as the lesser of NII and the $80,000 MAGI excess, resulting in $1,330", "The full $60,000 of passive income is subject to 3.8% NIIT without reduction for passive losses, resulting in $2,280", "No NIIT is owed because W-2 wages are not investment income and net passive income is below the threshold", "The NIIT applies to the full $80,000 MAGI excess regardless of income composition, resulting in $3,040"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under IRC Section 1411, net investment income includes passive income reduced by properly allocable deductions. Hoffman''s NII is $35,000 ($60,000 passive income less $25,000 passive loss). The 3.8% NIIT applies to the lesser of NII ($35,000) or MAGI exceeding the $200,000 threshold ($80,000), yielding $1,330. Choice D is incorrect because the NIIT applies to the lesser of NII or the MAGI excess, not automatically to the full excess amount.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q20: hard / L4 — Evaluate grouping election for RE professional with mixed-participation rentals
(6, 'Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules', 'Fitzpatrick, a qualifying real estate professional, owns three rental properties. Property A generates a $45,000 loss (700 hours). Property B generates a $50,000 loss (200 hours). Property C generates $20,000 income (50 hours). Without a grouping election, only Property A meets material participation. With a grouping election, aggregate hours total 950. Evaluate whether the grouping election produces a better tax outcome.', '["Grouping is worse because it dilutes Property A''s material participation by averaging hours across all properties", "Without grouping, all $95,000 of losses are nonpassive because real estate professional status alone eliminates passive treatment", "Grouping is better because aggregate 950 hours meets material participation, converting all three into a single nonpassive activity with a $75,000 deductible loss instead of leaving $30,000 suspended", "Grouping has no effect because the net tax outcome is identical whether grouped or separate"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under IRC Section 469(c)(7)(A), a real estate professional may elect to treat all rental real estate as a single activity. With grouping, Fitzpatrick''s 950 aggregate hours exceed the 500-hour threshold, making the combined net loss of $75,000 fully nonpassive and deductible. Without grouping, only Property A''s $45,000 is nonpassive, while Property B''s $50,000 loss exceeds Property C''s $20,000 passive income, leaving $30,000 suspended. Choice D is incorrect because grouping recovers the $30,000 that would otherwise be suspended.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q21: medium / L3 — Analyze at-risk recapture upon distribution reducing at-risk below zero
(6, 'Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules', 'Palmer invested $110,000 in an at-risk activity and deducted $70,000 of losses over three prior years, leaving an at-risk balance of $40,000. In 2026, Palmer receives a $55,000 cash distribution from the activity. Analyze the impact of this distribution on Palmer''s at-risk amount and tax liability.', '["The distribution has no effect on the at-risk amount because only losses reduce the at-risk balance", "The distribution reduces at-risk to $0, but the negative $15,000 is ignored until Palmer contributes additional capital", "The distribution reduces at-risk to $0, and the excess $15,000 reduces Palmer''s basis without creating taxable income", "Palmer must include $15,000 as ordinary income because the distribution reduces the at-risk amount below zero, triggering recapture under Section 465(e)"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under IRC Section 465(e), when a taxpayer''s at-risk amount is reduced below zero through distributions, changes in financing, or other events, the taxpayer must recapture previously deducted losses by including the negative amount as income. Palmer''s $55,000 distribution reduces the $40,000 at-risk balance to negative $15,000, requiring $15,000 of income recognition. Choice C is incorrect because the excess distribution is not a silent basis reduction — it triggers mandatory income recapture to the extent of previously allowed at-risk deductions.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q22: medium / L3 — Analyze substantial services exception to rental classification
(6, 'Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules', 'Cortez operates a furnished executive suite business providing tenants with reception services, mail handling, conference rooms, copying, and IT support. Average lease terms are 6 months. During 2026, the business generates a $40,000 loss, and Cortez participates for 600 hours. Analyze whether this activity is classified as a rental activity under the passive activity rules.', '["The activity is not a rental activity because significant personal services are provided in connection with the property use, so it is treated as a nonpassive trade or business", "The activity is a rental activity because the average lease term exceeds 7 days", "The activity is a rental activity regardless of services provided because it involves real property", "The activity is not a rental activity solely because Cortez participates for more than 500 hours"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under IRC Section 469 and Treas. Reg. 1.469-1T(e)(3)(ii)(B), an activity is not treated as a rental activity if significant personal services are provided in connection with making the property available for use. The executive suite services (reception, mail, IT) go beyond merely providing use of property. Because Cortez materially participates (600 hours > 500), the activity is nonpassive. Choice B is incorrect because the 7-day average period is a separate exception — the significant services exception applies regardless of lease duration.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q23: easy / L2 — Apply limited partner material participation restrictions
(6, 'Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules', 'Larson is a limited partner in a restaurant venture. During 2026, Larson participates in the activity for 130 hours. No other individual participates more than Larson. Under the Section 469 regulations, does Larson materially participate?', '["Yes, because Larson participated the most hours of any individual", "Yes, because 130 hours exceeds the 100-hour significant participation threshold", "No, because limited partners are permanently barred from material participation under all circumstances", "No, because limited partners may only use the 500-hour, 5-of-10-year, or personal service 3-year tests, and 130 hours fails the 500-hour threshold"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under IRC Section 469 and Treas. Reg. 1.469-5T(e), limited partners may establish material participation only through three tests: more than 500 hours of participation, material participation in 5 of the 10 preceding years, or 3 preceding years in a personal service activity. The test allowing participation exceeding all other individuals is not available to limited partners. Choice A is incorrect because Reg. 1.469-5T(a)(2) is specifically excluded for limited partners.', 'easy', 2),

-- Q24: medium / L2 — Apply qualified nonrecourse financing rules for real estate at-risk
(6, 'Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules', 'Harrison invests $60,000 cash in a real estate limited partnership. The partnership obtains a $340,000 mortgage from a commercial bank, secured solely by the real property, with no personal guarantee from any partner. Harrison''s allocable share of the mortgage is $85,000. Under Section 465(b)(6), what is Harrison''s total at-risk amount?', '["$60,000, because nonrecourse debt is never included in the at-risk amount", "$145,000, because qualified nonrecourse financing secured by real property from a bank is included in the at-risk amount", "$400,000, because all partnership debt is included for real estate activities", "$85,000, because only the nonrecourse debt share is counted and the cash contribution is excluded"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under IRC Section 465(b)(6), qualified nonrecourse financing is included in a taxpayer''s at-risk amount for real estate activities. The financing must be borrowed from a qualified lender (such as a bank), secured by real property, and no person may be personally liable. Harrison''s at-risk amount is $60,000 cash plus $85,000 share of qualified nonrecourse financing = $145,000. Choice A is incorrect because while nonrecourse debt is generally excluded, Section 465(b)(6) creates a specific exception for qualified nonrecourse real estate financing.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q25: medium / L2 — Apply rehabilitation credit within $25K active participation allowance
(6, 'Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules', 'Ortega actively participates in a rental property that generated rehabilitation tax credits of $8,000 and a rental loss of $12,000 during 2026. Ortega''s AGI is $105,000. Under Section 469(i), determine the allowable deduction and credit amounts.', '["$12,000 rental loss and $0 credits because passive credits cannot use the $25,000 allowance", "$0 rental loss and $0 credits because the phase-out eliminates the entire $25,000 allowance at AGI above $100,000", "$12,000 rental loss and $8,000 credit, both fully allowed within the $22,500 remaining after the $2,500 phase-out", "$12,000 rental loss but only $4,000 of credits because rehabilitation credits are subject to a 50% limitation"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under IRC Section 469(i), the $25,000 active participation allowance is reduced by 50% of AGI exceeding $100,000. At $105,000 AGI, the phase-out is $2,500, leaving $22,500. The $12,000 rental loss is applied first, leaving $10,500 of deduction-equivalent allowance that covers the $8,000 rehabilitation credit. Choice A is incorrect because Section 469(i)(2) specifically permits rehabilitation and low-income housing credits to use the active participation allowance after rental losses are applied.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q26: medium / L3 — Analyze proportional allocation of suspended passive losses among activities
(6, 'Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules', 'O''Brien has three passive activities in 2026. Activity A generates $30,000 income. Activity B generates a $50,000 loss. Activity C generates a $40,000 loss. After netting, $60,000 of total passive losses remain suspended. Analyze how the $60,000 of suspended losses is allocated between Activities B and C.', '["$33,333 to Activity B and $26,667 to Activity C, in proportion to their respective losses of $50,000 and $40,000", "$30,000 to Activity B and $30,000 to Activity C, divided equally regardless of loss size", "All $60,000 to Activity B because it has the larger loss", "$50,000 to Activity B and $10,000 to Activity C, based on original loss amounts less income absorbed"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under IRC Section 469 and Treas. Reg. 1.469-1T(f)(2), when passive losses are disallowed, the suspended amount is allocated among loss activities in proportion to each activity''s share of total passive losses. Activity B = $50,000/$90,000 x $60,000 = $33,333. Activity C = $40,000/$90,000 x $60,000 = $26,667. Choice B is incorrect because the allocation is proportional to each activity''s loss, not divided equally among loss-generating activities.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q27: easy / L1 — Definition of active participation standard
(6, 'Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules', 'Under IRC Section 469(i), what does the active participation standard require for the $25,000 rental loss allowance?', '["The taxpayer must work at least 750 hours per year in rental real estate activities", "The taxpayer must make management decisions such as approving tenants or setting rental terms, and own at least 10% of the activity", "The taxpayer must be a licensed real estate professional with documented rental hours", "The taxpayer must perform substantially all the work in the rental activity without hiring a property manager"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under IRC Section 469(i), active participation is a lower standard than material participation. It requires the taxpayer to participate in management decisions (approving tenants, setting terms, approving expenditures) and own at least 10% of the activity by value. A taxpayer can actively participate while employing a property manager, as long as decision-making authority is retained. Choice D is incorrect because active participation does not require performing substantially all the work — meaningful management involvement is sufficient.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q28: medium / L3 — Analyze significant participation activity income recharacterization
(6, 'Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules', 'Navarro participates in Activity D (a consulting venture) for 180 hours, generating $25,000 income, and Activity E (a retail shop) for 150 hours, generating $18,000 income. Navarro does not materially participate in either. Navarro also has $30,000 of passive losses from a limited partnership. Analyze how the significant participation activity recharacterization rule affects Navarro''s passive loss utilization.', '["The $43,000 of combined income fully offsets the $30,000 passive loss, leaving $13,000 of taxable passive income", "All $43,000 is treated as portfolio income exempt from passive netting rules", "The $43,000 is recharacterized as nonpassive income under the significant participation rules, preventing it from offsetting the $30,000 of passive losses", "The income retains its passive classification because recharacterization only applies when total significant participation hours exceed 500"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under IRC Section 469 and Treas. Reg. 1.469-2T(f)(2), net income from significant participation activities (100-500 hours without material participation) is recharacterized as nonpassive income. This prevents taxpayers from sheltering SPA income against passive losses from other activities. The $43,000 becomes nonpassive, and the $30,000 limited partnership loss remains suspended. Choice A is incorrect because the recharacterization removes the SPA income from the passive netting pool before it can absorb other passive losses.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q29: hard / L4 — Evaluate restructuring to qualify as real estate professional
(6, 'Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules', 'Wellington, filing single, works 1,600 hours as a financial analyst and spends 700 hours on three rental properties generating $85,000 in total losses. Wellington''s AGI is $280,000. She considers reducing analyst hours to 720 and increasing rental hours to 800 to qualify as a real estate professional. Her CPA notes this would meet both the 750-hour and more-than-50% requirements of Section 469(c)(7). Evaluate whether this restructuring strategy is advisable.', '["The strategy requires weighing the income reduction from cutting analyst hours against the $85,000 tax deduction, because lost compensation may exceed the tax benefit even at a 37% marginal rate", "The strategy is inadvisable because Wellington must materially participate in each property separately and none meets 500 hours individually", "The strategy is advisable because the $85,000 nonpassive deduction at a 37% marginal rate yields $31,450 in certain tax savings", "The strategy is advisable only if Wellington divests two properties and concentrates all hours in one"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under IRC Section 469(c)(7), Wellington would qualify as a real estate professional by spending 800 hours (exceeding 750) and more than 50% of personal services in real property trades. A grouping election would satisfy aggregate material participation. However, the tax savings of approximately $31,450 must be weighed against potentially substantial compensation lost by reducing analyst hours by 55%. Choice C is incorrect because it considers only the tax benefit while ignoring the income tradeoff — sound tax planning evaluates both sides.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q30: easy / L2 — Apply related-party exclusion for qualified nonrecourse financing
(6, 'Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules', 'Becker invests $40,000 cash in a real estate partnership and borrows $100,000 from her father on a nonrecourse basis, secured by the partnership''s real property. Under the at-risk rules, what is Becker''s at-risk amount?', '["$40,000, because nonrecourse financing from a related party does not qualify as qualified nonrecourse financing under Section 465(b)(6)", "$140,000, because the nonrecourse loan qualifies as qualified nonrecourse financing for real estate", "$100,000, because only borrowed amounts are included in the at-risk computation", "$70,000, calculated as $40,000 cash plus 30% of the related-party loan"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under IRC Section 465(b)(6), qualified nonrecourse financing for real estate must come from a qualified person who is not related to the taxpayer under Section 49(a)(1)(D)(iv). Because Becker''s father is a related party under Section 267(b)(1), the $100,000 nonrecourse loan is excluded from qualified nonrecourse financing. Only the $40,000 cash contribution is at risk. Choice B is incorrect because the related-party exclusion applies regardless of whether the loan is secured by real property.', 'easy', 2)
;

COMMIT;
